// =====================================================================
#heading(outlined: true)[3. Methodology]
// =====================================================================

*Markets and contract.* We reuse Sepp's four return processes: geometric
Brownian motion (GBM), a Merton jump-diffusion (JD), Heston stochastic
volatility (SV) and Heston with jumps (SVJ). Parameters are Sepp's own, so the
replication lines up with his tables. Every experiment sells one at-the-money
one-month call and hedges with the underlying. A proportional cost $c$ is
charged on the hedge trade, including the opening and closing trades, so a
strategy cannot hide a trade from the accounting.

*Sepp replication.* `hedging/sepp.py` implements the closed forms for the mean
coefficient $u$, the cost coefficient $c$, the local-variance term $p$ and the
variance floor $f$, then maximises
$S(N) = (u - c sqrt(N)) \/ (sqrt(T) sqrt(p\/N + f))$
over the number of rebalances $N$. The optimum maps to a price band and a delta
band. We check the coefficients against Sepp's Table 2 and the statistics
against a 60,000-path Monte Carlo simulation re-run at the analytic optimum.
Where they disagree, the formula is the candidate to debug; that is how the
jump-diffusion factor in Section 4.1 was found.

*Learned hedger.* `hedging/deep.py` is a PyTorch multilayer perceptron. The
state at each step is log-moneyness, time to maturity and current position; the
network outputs the position to hold next. Training minimises the conditional
value at risk (CVaR) of terminal P&L at tail fraction $alpha = 0.65$. The
learned hedger uses the same rollout as the classical strategies, so cost
accounting is identical on both sides.

*Benchmark and comparison.* The frozen benchmark is Heston at a 20 bp
proportional cost. The option is sold at 25% implied volatility against a 20%
long-run realised level. Without that volatility risk premium, refusing to hedge
dominates and a Sharpe comparison becomes meaningless, a failure mode we
observed and removed. The classical baseline is a delta band
(`delta_band = 0.1`) tuned once on a dedicated seed pool. For the head-to-head
map we cover four market models and five cost levels. The classical side is
re-tuned in every cell so a stale setting cannot manufacture a network win. The
network trains and is scored on disjoint seed pools, and an Optuna add-on
repeats four cells on a fourth, search-only pool.

*Overfitting controls.* Five seed pools (train, validation, tuning, search and
holdout) are disjoint. Every reported number averages three independent training
seeds, and we publish the per-seed minimum and win rate beside the mean. The
deep and classical P&L standard deviations are printed together, so a Sharpe
gain from under-hedging is visible. The holdout pool was simulated once, after
every choice was frozen. The correctness anchor is the frictionless check: at
zero cost the learned policy must recover the Black-Scholes delta.

// =====================================================================
#heading(outlined: true)[4. Results and discussion]
// =====================================================================

#heading(outlined: true)[4.1 Sepp replication]

The closed forms track Table 2. The largest relative error in $u$ is 3.9%, and
the GBM case I pair (0.5693 against 0.5692) and the SVJ case I pair (0.6551
against 0.6550) differ by 0.0001. The optimum is interior and moves to lower
frequency as the model gains complexity: extra trading cannot remove a variance
floor $f$, so paying more cost buys less. GBM case I optimises at 242 rebalances
with a Sharpe of 0.83; Heston case I at 75 with 0.43.

The jump-diffusion row needed a real fix. The JD branch had dropped a factor
$q = sqrt(pi^3)\/4$ that the GBM and SVJ branches carry. `scripts/diagnose_jd.py`
shows the second-moment gamma was correct to 0.2% all along; only the coefficient
was wrong. Restoring $q$ moves $p$ from 24% below the paper to 5.6% above it and
brings the analytic JD Sharpe (0.65) onto the simulated one (0.65) in case I.
The remaining case-II gap of about 12% in volatility is Sepp's own variance
approximation. The Monte Carlo cross-check passes for the other three models in
both mean and volatility.

#heading(outlined: true)[4.2 Deep hedging replication]

At zero cost the learned policy rediscovers the delta. Over 20,000 paths the
correlation between learned position and Black-Scholes delta is 0.9907, with
slope 1.148 and RMSE 0.0581; the mean learned position (0.499) is within 2% of
the mean delta (0.508).

The Heston experiment then reproduces the paper's qualitative claim. At zero
cost the network matches a delta hedge (Sharpe 1.154 against 1.139). As cost
rises it moves ahead of both every-step delta hedging and the frozen band.

